  "faqSchema": {
    "How does Singapore tax residency work (the 183-Day Rule)?": "You are considered a tax resident in Singapore if you are a Singapore Citizen, Permanent Resident, or a foreigner who stayed or worked in Singapore for at least 183 days in a calendar year. Under the Two-Year Concession, continuous employment across two calendar years qualifies you for resident tax rates for both years.",
    "What are the Singapore progressive income tax brackets for YA 2025 / YA 2026?": "Resident brackets range from 0% on the first S$20,000, progressing gradually through 2%, 3.5%, 7%, 11.5%, 15%, 18%, 19%, 20.5%, 22%, 23%, up to a top marginal rate of 24% for chargeable income exceeding S$1,000,000.",
    "How are foreign expats on Employment Passes (EP) and S Passes taxed in Singapore?": "Expats staying 183 days or more are taxed as residents (0% to 24%). Expats working 61 to 182 days are taxed at the higher of a flat 15% (no reliefs) or resident progressive rates. Expats working 60 days or fewer are exempt from income tax.",
    "What personal tax reliefs and deductions can residents claim in Singapore?": "Residents can claim Earned Income Relief, SRS contributions (up to S$15,300 for locals, S$35,700 for foreigners), Qualifying Child Relief, and 250% deductions on approved IPC donations, subject to an overall statutory cap of S$80,000 per YA.",
    "Is foreign-sourced income or capital gains taxable in Singapore?": "No. Singapore does not levy capital gains tax, and foreign-sourced income received by individuals in Singapore is generally exempt from tax under Section 13(7A) of the Income Tax Act.",
    "How does the Personal Income Tax Rebate apply in Singapore?": "In applicable budget years, IRAS grants a 50% Personal Income Tax Rebate capped at S$200 for resident taxpayers, automatically deducted from calculated tax."
  }
